meta {
  name: 11 - Get ERP Data (business fields only)
  type: http
  seq: 11
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/ocr/erp/{{docId}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-API-Key: {{apiKey}}
}

docs {
  The business data for one document, shaped for an ERP. Use this when you are
  writing to an ERP record rather than showing a document to a person.

  `docId` is set automatically by request 01 or 03.

  ## What it returns

      {
        "success": true,
        "response_format": "erp",
        "data": {
          "document_id": 1122,
          "request_id": "req_...",
          "source_file": "stock_july.xlsx",
          "document_type": "SPREADSHEET",
          "project_name": "VendorBillsApp",
          "extracted_at": "2026-09-30T04:59:38",
          "confidence": 1.0,
          "review_required": false,
          "data": { "sheet_title": "Sheet1" },
          "line_items": [
            { "Product": "Medicine A", "Qty": "10", "Price": "100.5" },
            { "Product": "Medicine B", "Qty": "20", "Price": "200.75" }
          ]
        }
      }

  ## Three differences from the full response

  **1. Line items are objects, not positional arrays.** The full response gives
  you `fields.columns` and `fields.rows` as two parallel lists, so you have to
  re-zip them - and any code that hardcodes `rows[i][2]` breaks the first time
  a column order changes. Here they are already joined, keyed by column name.

  Duplicate header names are suffixed (`Qty`, `Qty_2`, `Qty_3`) and blank ones
  become `column_N`, so no column is ever silently dropped.

  **2. No diagnostics.** `markdown`, `field_verification`,
  `classification_confidence`, `column_model_uncertain` and the page list are
  all absent. They are evidence *about* the extraction, for a human reviewer.
  What survives is the part you can route on:

  | Field | Use |
  |---|---|
  | `confidence` | 0.0-1.0 |
  | `review_required` | true = do not post unattended |
  | `review_fields` | present only when fields were flagged: the NAMES to hold |

  **3. Empty fields are omitted, not sent as `""` or `{}`.** The schema
  template contributes every key a document type could have; only the ones the
  document actually yielded appear. Read defensively - a key you saw on one
  document may be absent on the next.

  ## Values are verbatim

  No number parsing and no date reformatting. `"1,234.50"` stays that string,
  `"02/06/1990"` stays in whatever form the document printed. Typing and
  mapping are yours, because only you know your ERP's schema.

  ## The same payload, three other ways

  - `GET /api/v1/ocr/status/{request_id}?response_format=erp` - while polling
  - `GET /api/v1/ocr/document/{doc_id}?response_format=erp`
  - `POST /api/v1/ocr/process` with form field `response_format=erp`
    (synchronous only - the async submit has no data to shape yet)
  - `GET /api/v1/ocr/export/{doc_id}?format=erp` - the identical bytes as a
    download, so a mapping built from a saved sample holds when you go live

  An unrecognised `response_format` returns **400**, deliberately: silently
  serving the full envelope for a misspelled `erp` would look like the slim
  format does not work.

  ## Errors

  - 404 document not found
  - 409 still processing, or the job failed - poll `/status` first
}
